WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.930
The DC universe has plenty of shadowy vigilantes,
including about a dozen in Gotham City alone,

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.690
but few of them are as enigmatic as the Question.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:14.650
He's never been a household name, but that
doesn’t mean this is a character you shouldn’t

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.780
get to know.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:22.990
From two-fisted newsman to mysterious urban
shaman, here are the answers behind the Question.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:25.269
The Question is Always A

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:29.830
The Question was created by the legendary
Steve Ditko, who you may recognize as the

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:33.570
co-creator of Spider-Man and Doctor Strange,
among others.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:38.040
After leaving Marvel in the ‘60s due to
a dispute with Stan Lee, Ditko moved to a

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.450
smaller publisher called Charlton, where he
created the gadget-using 60's version of Blue

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:44.450
Beetle.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:49.560
Since Charlton needed a backup feature for
Blue Beetle, Ditko offered up the Question,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.730
although that wasn’t his original plan.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:56.120
Instead, it happened because he was concerned
that another character he’d created, Mr.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.420
A, would be censored by the Comics Code.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.910
In that respect, he was probably right.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:06.299
Mr. A was the end result of Ditko's growing
fascination with Objectivism.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:10.049
Conceived by Ayn [rhymes with “wine”]
Rand, it's the belief that moral truths exist

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:14.439
as absolutes, meaning that something that's
wrong is always wrong, no matter what the

00:01:14.439 --> 00:01:16.350
circumstances around it are.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:21.240
Any compromises, according to Rand, are moral
failings that only serve evil.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.950
Mr. A was a pure avatar of this philosophy,
right down to his name: “A is always A”

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:28.930
is Objectivism in a nutshell.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:34.079
Mr. A was ruthless in dispatching those he
deemed guilty of moral crimes, rising above

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:39.620
what Ditko called the “self-made neurotics”
that he saw in other, morally conflicted superheroes

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:41.069
like, say, Spider-Man.

00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:46.659
Either way, Ditko would continue creating
new Mr. A stories long after he left mainstream

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:50.170
comics, with the most recent appearing in
2009.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.999
With the Question, Ditko found a way to strike
a balance between representing his beliefs

00:01:54.999 --> 00:01:59.460
and the needs of Charlton Comics to get the
stamp of approval from the Comics Code.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.359
Reporter Vic Sage railed against injustice
and corruption, and when criminals would hide

00:02:04.359 --> 00:02:08.720
behind their money and connections, he would
dole out justice as the Question.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.510
Unlike Mr. A, however, the Question’s actions
were never quite ruthless enough to get banned

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.120
by the Code.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.430
Vic Sage and Victor Szasz

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.459
Along with the other Charlton Comics heroes,
the Question was acquired by DC Comics in

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:28.920
1983 and brought into the all-new DC Universe
after the reality-alterning Crisis On Infinite

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:29.920
Earths.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.650
In the new continuity, the creative team of
Dennis O'Neil and Denys Cowan began a new

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.840
Question series that redefined the character
for a new generation.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.560
This new Vic Sage wasn't originally Vic Sage
at all; he was an orphan in Hub City named

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.700
Charles Victor Szasz with no relation to Batman
villain Victor Zsasz who changed his name

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.030
to Vic Sage in order to pursue television
journalism.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:58.390
While the character retained some of the hallmarks
of Ditko's original conception, O’Neil and

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:02.100
Cowan’s version was, ironically, exactly
the kind of "self-made neurotic" that Ditko

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.849
had originally blasted when he created the
Question, used to explore philosophies that

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:08.860
went well beyond objectivism.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.770
O’Neil would even include a suggested reading
list in each issue’s letter column, often

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:17.950
diving into subjects like Zen Buddhism and
martial arts.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:19.560
The Rorschach Question

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:25.090
When the Charlton characters were acquired
by DC, writer Alan Moore and artist Dave Gibbons

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:30.739
pitched a miniseries that would revolve around
them, dealing with themes of violence, superheroism,

00:03:30.739 --> 00:03:32.439
and modern political issues.

00:03:32.439 --> 00:03:37.120
DC editorial was impressed with the pitch,
but worried about where the ending of the

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.549
book left the characters for future use.

00:03:39.549 --> 00:03:44.370
Moore revised the pitch, creating new characters
that were inspired by the Charlton heroes,

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.739
leaving the originals to join the DC Universe
proper.

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:53.540
That book would go on to become the groundbreaking
Watchmen, and Rorschach, one of the main standouts

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.980
in the book, was obviously inspired by the
Question.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:01.890
He was both more and less faithful to Ditko's
original conception, committed to seeing the

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:07.349
world in absolute terms of black-and-white,
good and bad, directly following in Ditko's

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:08.670
Objectivist footsteps.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:14.120
Here, however, the Question's featureless
face is replaced with a Rorschach test inkblot

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:19.049
that's different every time it appears, subtly
mocking the hard-edged worldview that Rorschach

00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:20.459
operates under.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:25.810
Years before DC and Watchmen would officially
cross over (against Moore's wishes), Rorschach

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.060
and the Question actually had a crossover
sort of.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:35.270
In The Question #17, Vic Sage travels to Seattle
to follow some assassins, and since it’s

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:38.870
a long flight, he reads a copy of Watchmen
to pass the time.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:43.690
When he falls asleep, the Question remembers
a prior moment from earlier issues, with his

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.770
body replaced by Rorschach's.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:51.000
It all comes to a head when copying Rorschach's
"punch-first, worry later" strategy ends with

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.860
Vic Sage stumbling through the snow trying
to escape two goons that captured him.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.230
The Question learns his lesson, especially
considering Rorschach’s difficulty escaping

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:02.560
from dangerous situations in the snow.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:03.830
His conclusion?

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:05.970
“Rorschach sucks.”

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.100
The Urban Shaman

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:13.990
A 2005 miniseries reinvented the Question
as a quasi-mystical “urban shaman” capable

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:17.770
of communicating with cities and manipulating
the flow of chi.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:22.090
In this new version of the character, the
chemicals released in the Question's belt

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:27.840
were also hallucinogens, giving Vic Sage the
ability to "walk between two worlds" and a

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.740
dreamier outlook than he’d ever had before.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:35.460
Written by Rick Veitch and drawn by Tommy
Lee Edwards, the miniseries showcased Vic

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:41.099
Sage helping the city of Metropolis itself
foil Lex Luthor's plot to build a chi-powered

00:05:41.099 --> 00:05:44.520
building that would (what else?) kill Superman.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:50.910
Luthor's plan also involved the Subterraneans,
arrogant super-thieves who saw operating undetected

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:54.510
in Metropolis as the biggest thrill a criminal
could achieve.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:59.940
The Subterraneans would only rob banks when
Superman was half a planet away, and ran an

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:05.220
entire vice ring out of the bathrooms in Metropolis,
since that’s the one place Superman wouldn’t

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.710
peek with his X-Ray vision.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:11.980
With the help of a ghost, because why not,
the Question was able to kill the Subterraneans

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:16.680
and trap their souls within the grounds of
the chi-powered building, rendering it incapable

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.880
of shooting lethal lasers.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:21.349
No weird deed goes unpunished, though.

00:06:21.349 --> 00:06:26.050
Superman kicked the Question out of Metropolis
for killing people, and for having a bit of

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:29.129
a creepy crush on Lois Lane.

00:06:29.129 --> 00:06:30.190
Renee Montoya

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:35.419
While Vic Sage is the most well-known Question,
he's not the only one.

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:40.960
After quitting the Gotham City Police Department,
detective Renee Montoya took over the identity,

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.550
becoming Vic Sage’s protege.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:47.430
This iteration of the Question stuck close
to O'Neil's conception of the character, with

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:53.050
a mysterious Vic Sage teaching Montoya to
follow her curiosity, and even sending her

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:55.280
to the heroes that had trained him to fight.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:56.280
The reason?

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.800
Sage was dying of lung cancer and wanted Montoya
to take over when he died.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.210
She did, and in addition to the usual crooks,
Montoya tangled with the Religion of Crime,

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:11.930
a fanatical sect of criminals who believed
that Cain (of Cain and Abel fame) was a prophet

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:12.930
of sin.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:17.030
Eventually, Montoya accidentally killed their
former leader in a fight to the death, leading

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:20.090
the cult to worship her as "The Faceless One."

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:25.610
As for Vic, he was briefly reanimated during
Blackest Night, a crossover that saw dead

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:29.259
superheroes brought back to life as superpowered
zombies.

00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:33.210
Fortunately for everyone, that was only temporary.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.479
New 52 and Rebirth

00:07:35.479 --> 00:07:41.610
When DC Comics rebooted their entire universe
in 2011, The Question got a major facelift

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:46.820
as part of the Trinity of Sin, a group of
characters cursed with immortality and doomed

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:50.139
to try and endlessly atone for their heinous
sins.

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:56.400
Five years later, though, DC again rebooted
their entire universe, and in 2018 they announced

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.660
the debut of yet another new version of the
character.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:01.139
So will this one stick?

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:03.900
With this character, there are never any answers.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.680
Only… more Questions.

